# Functions with order zero at every place are constant

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.mem_range_algebraMap_of_forall_ord_eq_zero_of_isAlgClosed`
(`4e728b40-ba0e-5edc-885d-a8df4da58b96`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `39d7436f-3d6b-5ed7-b84c-f746bfa836ad`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and suppose $K$ is algebraically closed. Let $j
\in F$ be transcendental over $K$, and assume $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field
$K(j) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K {j}`, so that $F$ is a function field of one variable over $K$.
Here a place of $F/K$ (the structure
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22)) consists of a valuation
subring of $F$ that contains the image of $K$ under `algebraMap K F`, is not the whole of $F$, and
is a principal ideal ring; for such a $v$ the quantity `v.ord f` is the integer $-\log$ of the value
at $f$ of the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation attached to the height-one prime of that
valuation ring, i.e. the normalised order of vanishing of $f$ at $v$. The assertion is that if $x
\in F$ satisfies $\operatorname{ord}_v(x) = 0$ for every place $v$ of $F/K$, then $x$ lies in the
range of `algebraMap K F`, that is, $x$ is a constant.

This is the classical statement that on a complete curve over an algebraically closed field a
rational function with neither zeros nor poles is constant (equivalently, the only
everywhere-regular invertible functions on a one-dimensional function field are the elements of the
constant field). It is used throughout the treatment of divisors and the divisor class group of a
function field, for instance in the rigidity statement that an automorphism fixing every place is
the identity, and in comparisons of orders under constant field extension.

